  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.31.0 release assets and hashed independently, then checked again by re-fetching the URL this pin itself names. Only the pegasus artifact changed: install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.28.0 and earlier. v5.31.0 is the release this distribution has been waiting for without knowing it: a server now declares the agents it reaches, instead of every agent declaring the servers it wants. Until now the relation lived in each agent's front matter, so adding a server meant editing engine-owned agent files, which this repository must not copy. A distribution can now ship an MCP server of its own by adding two files under its own content overlay and modifying none. Verified by execution on the engine side, not asserted. Nothing rendered changed: the full catalogue with every server selected produces the same digest entry for entry as the previous release, so this distribution's build output is unaffected. One residue belongs to whoever writes such a descriptor here, and it is not a defect but a consequence: the reaches list names agents by name, and this distribution renames agents, so a descriptor added by the overlay must name the names as they exist AFTER the rebrand transform, never the engine ones. The engine's own descriptors are safe because the transform rewrites their front matter along with their bodies, which was checked by running it. A name that does not match refuses loudly at content load, which is the good failure; a new engine agent that a local descriptor never names is the quiet one, and nothing reports it. The same release closes a silence rather than a defect, reported from real use: an agent asked to edit a file on another host refused, naming an instruction it believed was its own configuration. It was the description of a tool it does not have, inside a runtime prompt it never receives, because a product that renders its own agent prompts replaces the runtime's per-model one rather than inheriting it. The shared system prompt now states that a remote edit goes through the shell because no local edit tool can reach another host, that a runtime denial still stands, and that an agent must quote a rule and name its file before claiming to be blocked by it. This distribution changed nothing of its own, as in 1.6.0, 1.7.0 and 1.8.0: the work happened upstream and only the pin moved."
  }
